index 0000000..53796cb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/HEGEL_REPORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,142 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: reed-solomon-erasure
+
+Crate: `reed-solomon-erasure` 6.0.0 (Reed-Solomon erasure coding over GF(2^8) and GF(2^16)).
+Tests added with `hegeltest` 0.28.2 (`#[hegel::test]`), supplementing the existing quickcheck
+suite and fuzz targets. No AI-contribution-gating file was found in the repository
+(no `HUMAN_VS_MACHINE.md` / `.ai-ack` / `CONTRIBUTING.md`; README/LICENSE checked).
+All work is local; nothing was pushed or upstreamed.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+All hegel tests live in the existing test files (`src/tests/mod.rs`,
+`src/tests/galois_16.rs`, and the `tests` module of `src/galois_8.rs`),
+prefixed `hegel_`. Erasure subsets are drawn via a Fisher-Yates shuffle so every
+choice is shrinkable — the existing quickcheck tests pick corruption positions with
+`rand::random`, which hegel/quickcheck cannot shrink, and they never exercise more
+than `parity` erasures.
+
+1. **`hegel_encode_erase_reconstruct_roundtrip`** (crown, `src/tests/mod.rs`) —
+   draw `k, m ∈ 1..=10`, shard length `1..=64`, full `0..=255` shard bytes; encode;
+   erase a drawn subset of size `0..=m` (any mix of data and parity positions);
+   `reconstruct` must recover every shard exactly and `verify` must pass.
+   Evidence: the crate's core contract, its README, the quickcheck
+   `qc_encode_verify_reconstruct_verify`, and `fuzz/fuzz_targets/fuzz_encode_reconstruct.rs`.
+   Exploratory-run counters confirmed the erasure branch is not vacuous: 820 of 1000
+   cases had at least one erasure.
+2. **`hegel_reconstruct_too_many_erasures_fails_cleanly`** — erase a drawn subset of
+   size `m+1..=n`; `reconstruct` must return `Err(Error::TooFewShardsPresent)` and
+   (per its doc: "if the method returns an `Error`, then nothing is touched") leave
+   every shard bit-identical, erased slots still `None`. Not covered by the existing
+   suite (quickcheck caps corruption at `parity`; only one fixed-shape unit test).
+3. **`hegel_reconstruct_data_recovers_only_data_shards`** — after erasing `≤ m`
+   shards, `reconstruct_data` restores all data shards exactly, leaves erased parity
+   shards `None`, and leaves present parity shards untouched. Evidence: doc comment on
+   `reconstruct_data` ("Reconstructs only the data shards") and the `data_only` branch
+   of `reconstruct_internal`.
+4. **`hegel_verify_detects_single_byte_corruption`** — `verify` is true for a freshly
+   encoded set; after XOR-ing one drawn byte of one drawn shard (data *or* parity) with
+   a drawn non-zero delta, `verify` is false. Evidence: MDS property of the systematic
+   Vandermonde construction (every parity coefficient non-zero, so any single-symbol
+   change perturbs every parity row). Stronger than `qc_verify`, which corrupts whole
+   shards with unconstrained random bytes.
+5. **`hegel_encode_deterministic_regardless_of_parity_prefill`** — two independently
+   constructed codecs with the same `(k, m)` produce identical parity for the same data,
+   one starting from zeroed parity buffers and one from drawn garbage (doc: "You do not
+   need to clear the parity shards beforehand"); re-encoding is idempotent.
+6. **`hegel_encode_is_columnwise`** (shard-size independence) — parity byte at column
+   `j` of an encoding of length-`L` shards equals the parity of encoding just column `j`
+   as 1-byte shards. Evidence: `mul_slice`/`mul_slice_add` operate element-wise, so the
+   code is a per-byte-position linear map; a SIMD/unrolling bug would break this.
+7. **`hegel_single_data_shard_parity_equals_data`** (k = 1 edge, `m ∈ 1..=255`) —
+   with one data shard, every parity shard is an exact copy of it. Evidence:
+   `Matrix::vandermonde` entry `[r][0] = nth(r)^0 = 1` and the 1×1 top submatrix `[1]`,
+   so the whole encode matrix is ones.
+8. **`hegel_single_parity_recovers_any_single_erasure`** (m = 1 edge, `k ∈ 1..=255`) —
+   a single parity shard recovers any one erased shard (data or parity) at any drawn
+   position, up to the GF(2^8) shard-count limit.
+9. **`hegel_mul_slice_matches_scalar_mul`** and **`hegel_mul_slice_xor_matches_scalar_mul`**
+   (`src/galois_8.rs`) — backend differential: the slice routines (pure-Rust 4-way
+   unrolled by default, SIMD C under `simd-accel`) must agree with the scalar table
+   lookup `mul` for every coefficient and length `1..=300` (covers the unroll main
+   loop, the scalar tail, and with `simd-accel` the SIMD-block + remainder split).
+   This is the "feature paths agree" property, run under both builds. **Found a real
+   bug at length 0 — see Suspected bugs.**
+10. **`hegel_mul_div_roundtrip`** (`src/galois_8.rs`) — `div(mul(a, b), b) == a` for
+    `b ≠ 0`, tying `div` to `mul` directly (existing tests only check `mul(a, div(1,a)) == 1`).
+11. **`hegel_encode_erase_reconstruct_roundtrip_gf16`** (`src/tests/galois_16.rs`) —
+    the crown property over GF(2^16) (`[u8; 2]` elements), whose arithmetic is a
+    completely different code path (polynomial reduction, no lookup tables).
+
+Plus **`mul_slice_empty_input_does_not_panic`** (`src/galois_8.rs`) — deterministic
+pin for the empty-slice SIMD bug below (plain `#[test]`; no randomness needed).
+
+## Test results
+
+- Default build: **all 112 lib tests + 7 doctests pass**, including all 12 hegel tests
+  and the entire pre-existing quickcheck/unit suite (`cargo test`).
+- Liveness check: temporarily inverting the crown assertion produced a fully shrunk
+  counterexample (`k=1, m=1, shard_len=1, data=[0], erase_count=0`); reverted.
+- Exploratory 10x run (`test_cases = 1000` on all 12 tests, with case counters):
+  all green; crown test confirmed 1000 cases executed, 820 with ≥ 1 erasure.
+  Counters and elevated counts reverted; committed tests use the default 100 cases.
+- `cargo test --features simd-accel`: all hegel property tests pass (the SIMD backend
+  agrees with the scalar oracle on all non-empty inputs); exactly one test fails,
+  deterministically — the known-failure pin `mul_slice_empty_input_does_not_panic`.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+**`galois_8::mul_slice` / `galois_8::mul_slice_xor` panic on empty slices under
+`--features simd-accel` (backend divergence).**
+
+- Repro: `cargo test --features simd-accel mul_slice_empty_input_does_not_panic`
+  (or call `reed_solomon_erasure::galois_8::mul_slice(5, &[], &mut [])` in any
+  program built with `simd-accel` on x86_64/aarch64).
+- Panic: `index out of bounds: the len is 0 but the index is 0`, `src/galois_8.rs:297`
+  (and `:319` for the xor variant).
+- Code path: the pure-Rust implementations guard with `return_if_empty!(len)`
+  (`src/galois_8.rs:137-144, 179-186`), but the `simd-accel` wrappers take raw
+  pointers via `&input[0]` / `&mut out[0]` **before** any length check
+  (`pub fn mul_slice` at `src/galois_8.rs:291-305`, `pub fn mul_slice_xor` at
+  `:313-327`), so they panic where the default build returns silently. These are
+  `pub` functions in a `pub` module, so the divergence is user-visible. The
+  higher-level `ReedSolomon` API is unaffected (it rejects empty shards with
+  `Error::EmptyShard` before reaching these routines).
+- Status: NOT fixed (per instructions). Pinned deterministically by
+  `mul_slice_empty_input_does_not_panic`, which passes on the default build and fails
+  under `simd-accel`. The two sibling differential properties exclude length 0 with a
+  comment referencing the pin, so they stay green and keep testing everything else.
+- Found by: the hegel differential tests, which drew `len = 0` and shrank to the
+  minimal case on the first `simd-accel` run.
+
+No other property failed in any run (default and `simd-accel`, default and 10x counts).
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None abandoned outright. One property was consciously *not* attempted: asserting a
+specific parity formula for `m = 1` (e.g. "single parity = XOR of data shards") — the
+systematic Vandermonde construction does not promise all-ones parity rows for `k > 1`,
+so only the reconstruction contract was asserted. The k = 1 parity-copy property was
+asserted only after deriving it from `Matrix::vandermonde` (`nth(r)^0 = 1`).
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The porting guidance ("existing PBTs use narrow ranges because shrinking was
+  slow — broaden") mapped well here: the quickcheck suite's corruption positions come
+  from `rand::random` inside the property (unshrinkable) and never exceed `parity`;
+  drawing the erasure subset through hegel and adding the `> m` failure-path property
+  were exactly the gaps.
+- The Fisher-Yates draw-a-subset idiom (suggested by the "avoid rejection sampling /
+  construct valid inputs directly" section) worked perfectly for "erase WHICH shards";
+  a `vecs(...).unique(true).min_size(e)` approach would have risked heavy rejection
+  when `e ≈ n`. A short cookbook entry for "draw a k-subset / permutation" would save
+  others deriving it.
+- The "feature flag testing" row in the catalogue paid off immediately: the only real
+  bug found lives exclusively behind `simd-accel`. Worth strengthening that advice to
+  "run the finished suite once per compile-time backend feature" — the bug is invisible
+  in the default build no matter how many cases run.
+- The Run-and-Reflect requirement to verify case counts with a counter (not wall-clock)
+  was useful: the 10x run also revealed that `k ∈ 1..=255` codec construction dominates
+  runtime (Vandermonde build + O(k³) inversion per case), which informed keeping the
+  m = 1 test at the default case count rather than shrinking its input range.
+- Gotcha #11 (delete `.hegel/` after deliberate liveness failures) avoided a confusing
+  replay after the inverted-assertion check.
